{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'resend|append|duplicate|budget|maximum|fragment' docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | head -55; sed -n '194,250p' docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; rg -n 'max|budget|limit|pending|expired' src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"51:- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n68:  `append!` carries one value toward the other end and a reader positioned\n102:                                       ; :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n106: :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n164:  fixed mapping: `cursor` and `next` need `:reader`, `append!` needs\n199:`chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n202:   found in the table. Either absent: append the `not-found` answer with\n203:   the name and no identity. Otherwise append the `descriptor` answer of\n206:1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append the answer\n209:   mapping: append the answer with\n213:   `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and `:dao.stream/append!` to it\n216:   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`. The budget\n221:For `:dao.stream/next` with a budget `k` the mirror may follow the successor\n227:budget is correct. A value the channel cannot carry is answered with\n253:`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n258:surface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n282:- `append!` `v`: send the request and return the channel writer's outcome.\n285:  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n288:  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n292:reflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n309:abandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n312:`append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n349:relayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n352:### 2.5 Loss and resend\n354:The reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n358:three operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n359:or a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n361:append whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n362:event writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n370:A channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n372:`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n381:wired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n385:vocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget unbounded.\n392:keeps the events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends the\n422:- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n423:  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n425:- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n433:  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n436:  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n470:`append!` is reported `append-unknown`, and reattachment is the caller's,\n473:as a source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside\n474:an outer `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n500:both ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel a peer appends\n503:and appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h :port p}\n552:mints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n600:beside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n616:**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n651:codec, encryption and fragmentation in that order. Key distribution is\n### 2.3 The mirror step\n\n`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`, and\n`(mirror-step table names chan-reader cursor chan-writer)` with the name\nmap; without one every name is unmapped. For each request read from\n`chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n\n0. A named request: look up its name in the name map and the identity\n   found in the table. Either absent: append the `not-found` answer with\n   the name and no identity. Otherwise append the `descriptor` answer of\n   step 3 for that entry, with its own `:dao.stream/identity` and the name\n   under `:dao.stream.remote/name`. Steps 1 to 4 do not run for it.\n1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append the answer\n   with `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/not-found`.\n2. Present, but the op outside the entry's declared surface under 2.1's\n   mapping: append the answer with\n   `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`.\n3. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   directly, adding `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; and apply\n   `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and `:dao.stream/append!` to it\n   through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request`\n   (`dao.stream.middleware.md`), with context\n   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`. The budget\n   chase below runs the same `next` through the same path.\n4. Append the answer with `:dao.stream.remote/id` and\n   `:dao.stream/identity`.\n\nFor `:dao.stream/next` with a budget `k` the mirror may follow the successor\ncursor of each `ok` outcome up to `k - 1` further times and place the\nfurther outcome maps, in order, under `:dao.stream.remote/more`. The first\noutcome is the answer map itself. The vector ends at and includes the first\nnon-`ok` outcome when one occurs, so `blocked`, `end` and `gap` reach the\nreader with the source's own recovery cursor. A mirror that ignores the\nbudget is correct. A value the channel cannot carry is answered with\n`:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/oversize` in place of that\nelement (2.1).\n\nSteps 0 to 4 are the mirror's only contacts with the entry's handle: no\nanswer path calls a handle outside `apply-request`, so nothing bypasses the\nentry's middleware or its declared surface, and a reflection mints every\nanchor, `:oldest` and `:newest` included, by request.\n\nThe mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\noutcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\nrests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\naccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\na composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n\n### 2.4 The reflection\n\n`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\nreflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\ncontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\nThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\nshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\nwriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n22:   `max-datagram` bytes, fragment envelope included -- the figure\n24:   encoded message over that budget is split into fragment envelopes,\n38:   `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB) refuses an\n40:   and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest\n75:(def max-datagram\n76:  \"The whole encoded datagram's budget, fragment envelope included: the\n82:(def default-max-message-bytes\n83:  \"64 KiB, the default reassembly maximum (3.2).\"\n87:(def default-max-partial-messages\n146:  (chunks/split payload max-datagram\n162:   datagram layer, as the Base64 closure appending one outbound value to\n168:   reports lost datagrams -- `port-step!` requires it. `:max-message-bytes`\n169:   and `:max-partial-messages` are the two reassembly bounds (defaults\n171:  [{:keys [send! traffic raw-traffic max-message-bytes\n172:           max-partial-messages]}]\n185:         :max-message-bytes (or max-message-bytes\n186:                                default-max-message-bytes)\n187:         :max-partial-messages (or max-partial-messages\n188:                                   default-max-partial-messages)\n206:   a single datagram when it fits the budget, fragments when it does\n208:   exceeds :dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes -- never sent, torn or\n215:      (> n (:max-message-bytes @port))\n219:      (<= n max-datagram)\n285:                       (:max-message-bytes @port)\n286:                       (:max-partial-messages @port))]\n356:   dropping every other attachment's, appending each kept value onto\n388:(def default-step-budget\n389:  \"The default `port-step!` budget: raw values consumed in one step when\n410:   read to `blocked` from the port's own raw cursor, bounded by `budget`\n419:  ([port] (port-step! port default-step-budget))\n420:  ([port budget]\n425:     (loop [left budget]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
