{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-06 16:39:39 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-06 23:39:39 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Consensus: use TCP WebSocket now, with transport selection, supervision, and resource enforcement below `dao.stream`. Completing this milestone includes removing transport knowledge from both the REPL and linker compositions.**\n\nThe [consensus report](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791304310868-architect-transport-mob-consensus.gpt-6-astra.findings.md) records the evidence, review rounds, session IDs, and acceptance criteria. This was an architecture review: no implementation changes or runtime tests.\n\n**How consensus was reached**\n\nI reviewed the source and challenged Fable’s earlier recommendations through three Fable rounds and two DeepSeek rounds.\n\n| Disagreement | Resolution and reason |\n|---|---|\n| Watch WebSocket projection cursors from `yin.repl.dht` | Fable withdrew this. Transport supervision stays below `dao.stream`; arbitrary incoming traffic does not prove a request was answered. |\n| Expose a health accessor for Yin to monitor | DeepSeek withdrew this. The stream layer reports ordinary loss; Yin decides whether and when to reconnect. |\n| Evict the idlest session to admit newcomers | Reject newcomers after reaping expired sessions. New connections must not continually displace healthy readers. |\n| Defer outbound byte bounds | Fable withdrew this after the non-reading-client counterexample: a client can keep requesting indefinitely without becoming idle. |\n| Defer REPL migration | Both agreed that this would leave the owner’s invariant unfinished. |\n| Reuse retry counts or wait for draining to finish before expiry | Rejected: retries reset counts, observers inflate polling, and continuous traffic can prevent draining from finishing. |\n\nFable’s [final reconciliation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791304310868-architect-transport-mob-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md) and DeepSeek’s [reconciliation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791304310868-architect-transport-mob-r2.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md) support the resulting positions.\n\n**D1 — Make the boundary complete**\n\nGeneralize the transport machinery in `yin.vm.linker.head.ws` into a stepped composition under `dao.stream`. Keep board naming, its read-only exposure table, and domain repair in Yin.\n\nMove descriptor construction, socket seams, sessions, framing, transport selection, and channel supervision below that boundary. Apply the same migration to `yin.repl.serve`, `yin.repl.connect`, and transport-specific host glue. Renaming `head.ws` alone is insufficient.\n\nUse existing explicit dispatch and ordinary control/lifecycle streams. Add no global registry, application callbacks, autonomous timer loop, or core stream operation.\n\n**D2 — TCP now; contract-preserving UDP later**\n\nChoose WebSocket over TCP. The existing UDP machinery does not remove the deferred return-path proof and amplification-control work needed for board exposure on the DHT socket.\n\nUDP substitution must preserve:\n\n- Handle operations and outcome sets.\n- Source identity, opaque cursors, and name resolution.\n- Declared read/write surfaces.\n- Source retention gaps and recovery cursors.\n- Request correlation, close/loss behavior, and append ambiguity.\n\n“Zero breakage” means unchanged consumer code and contract—not identical latency or exactly-once delivery. Existing append deduplication remains payload-owned. Writable REPL behavior needs explicit loss/duplication tests before UDP is considered substitutable.\n\n**D3 — Bounds belong below the boundary, but span several layers**\n\nThe fixes do **not** belong exclusively in `ws-project` and `remote`.\n\n| Owner | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Channel composition / `ws-project` | Session admission, pending and idle expiry, fair scheduling, session isolation, shutdown |\n| `remote` | Bounded mirror/read/retry loops, locally clamped request budgets, bounded retained request/answer state |\n| Transport and host adapters | Frame limits, pre-adoption queues, decoding/reassembly limits, outbound byte bounds, effective teardown |\n\nUse finite defaults. Reject new sessions at capacity after reaping expired ones. Bound pending and closing resources as well as active sessions.\n\nFor liveness, the **stream-layer supervisor** observes correlated protocol-response progress, using explicitly supplied time and fixed request deadlines. A remote `blocked` answer proves responsiveness; a locally generated `blocked` does not. Neither an unchanged head cursor nor unrelated traffic establishes channel health.\n\nOn expiry, report existing stream loss semantics. Yin receives `:source-lost` and applies its repair policy. This neither invalidates the installed head nor claims to detect withholding or freshness.\n\nProcess queued replies with bounded work/grace; never let continuous traffic postpone expiry indefinitely. Correct `:answered` reporting accordingly.\n\nA host without enforceable byte bounds and teardown remains gated. Rate limits plus idle eviction are not a memory bound.\n\n**D4 — Automatic advertisement for the common case**\n\nUse this precedence:\n\n1. Explicit advertised endpoint.\n2. Concrete bind address.\n3. For wildcard binds, a unique eligible interface address obtained through host effect/result streams; an available route hint may refine selection.\n\nIf the result remains ambiguous, keep the node usable but withhold the misleading token and identify one override option. Never advertise `0.0.0.0` or `::`.\n\nThis removes operator work on an unambiguous LAN setup. It cannot infer arbitrary NAT mappings or guarantee public reachability. Format IPv6 correctly below the transport boundary, and publish the token only after the required listeners are confirmed bound.\n\n**D5 — Keep four stages, preceded by a small contract step**\n\n| Stage | Deliverable |\n|---|---|\n| S0 | Boundary contract and transport-substitution acceptance criteria |\n| S1 | Session/admission bounds, expiry, fairness, failure isolation |\n| S2 | All nested loop/state budgets, inbound/outbound byte bounds, effective teardown |\n| S3a | Neutral board composition, stream liveness, shutdown and lifecycle-gap handling |\n| S3b | REPL serve/connect/host migration |\n| S4 | Advertisement, IPv6, host verification, documentation, then gate lift |\n\nS3 may land in separate commits, but milestone sign-off waits for both parts.\n\nRequired tests include continuous flooding, request-without-reading, half-open connections, healthy quiet boards, missing close callbacks, restart identity changes, transport substitution, IPv6, and real JVM/Node/Dart sockets.\n\nRemaining limits are explicit: cleartext first-contact replay, withholding, no guaranteed progress under hostile trace alternation, NAT reachability, and possible false channel loss under overload. Numerical resource profiles are engineering choices to validate—not additional decisions for the owner."}}
